@liggitt Thanks, got it. But, Is it better to put this code outside the loop?
```
if r.timeout > 0 {
    if r.ctx == nil {
        r.ctx = context.Background()
    }
    var cancelFn context.CancelFunc
    r.ctx, cancelFn = context.WithTimeout(r.ctx, r.timeout)
    defer cancelFn()
}
for {
    ...... // retry logic
}
```
So that, the code is more clear and eliminate useless context.WithTimeout.

yes, moving the timeout-building context part out of the loop makes sense. it would also make sense to check if the context has an error before making the request and returning early

```go
for {
  ...
  if r.ctx != nil {
    if err := r.ctx.Err(); err != nil {
      // short-circuit if we've already been cancelled or timed-out
      return err
    }
    req = req.WithContext(r.ctx)
  }
  ...
}
```
